From b0ea389f8bb140e7d636a239bc4298f89635a945 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 15:02:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Review fixes: fail closed on incomplete turns, never lose a
 paid response
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Five findings from a review of the transport commit.

assertUsable let pause_turn, tool_use and stop_sequence through as usable.
The first two mean the turn is not over, so the text is a fragment the model
meant to continue — and a fragment of a finding reads exactly like a
finding. Inverted to an allowlist on end_turn, so a stop reason added to the
API later fails closed instead of arriving usable by default. New
IncompleteResponseError distinguishes it from truncation and refusal.

RecordingTransport called its sink unguarded, so a read-only directory or a
full disk destroyed a response that had already been received and paid for.
This is the one place in the package where a failure must not propagate: the
sink is now guarded, the failure is reported loudly through an injectable
handler, and the caller still gets what the model said. The report says
explicitly that the response was not recorded, so a later miss is not a
mystery.

Neither thinking nor effort was validated anywhere. Requests arrive from
cassette files, which are JSON and can say anything, and the transport maps
thinking with a two-way branch — so an unrecognised value silently became
'disabled', a request that quietly stopped asking for reasoning and would
replay that way forever. Validation goes in resolveRequest, the single choke
point both the hash and the API call pass through, and assertCassette
re-raises it with the offending file named. outputSchema is shape-checked
too.

The core-purity lint rule was not extended with @mae/runtime, so an
invariant the README says is enforced twice was enforced once. Core reaching
the model seam is the worst version of that violation: the charter validator
exists to sit between the model and the record, and it cannot do that from
behind one.

renderCassetteLibrary had no test and CI never ran --project cli at all.
Both fixed; the new tests cover the authored-content warning being earned
rather than unconditional.

Not changed: the review also flagged {thinking:'disabled', effort:'xhigh'}
as an API-rejected combination. I have no documentation for that constraint,
and encoding an unverified 400 rule would make the transport reject requests
the API may well accept — the failure mode the claim classifiers are
explicitly written to avoid.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
